#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407918 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152203 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "99c2db8c-670b-521b-8942-3b89f1e95d3f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Injections For Functions Whose Domain or Codomain Are Real Intervals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27143f05-01a0-516c-bb47-d27121720b6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407891} {:answer/id #uuid "3d4e93b6-1460-51b3-854b-d70d0185543b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407894} {:answer/id #uuid "5f61eda3-2ef3-5e67-a98c-6effd83ff8d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407897} {:answer/id #uuid "76f0dd4e-ff74-50db-9f41-52a58331f2e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407899} {:answer/id #uuid "9b94f507-0ee4-574c-849e-434a6fd9142d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407905}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b94f507-0ee4-574c-849e-434a6fd9142d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407905} :answer-field/id #uuid "6472838c-210f-5ad0-99a2-67a0bbd5ad6b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407914}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1a3dbd42-cd16-5083-8842-33f0028feb3d" :question/math-academy-id "q-105207" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:[-2,4]\\to [-3,9]$ which is given by $f(x)=2x+1?$\n\n- $f(-2)=f(4)$\n- $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in [-2,4],$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}$\n- $f$ is an injection" :question/worked-solution "First, we graph the function.\n\n![](images/5f/5fd931a87d6a5fb32d10ed931f443a8175f0760eba9fe2fe69336f8f79e9fd68.png)\n\nA function $f:X\\to Y$ is an injection (also known as *one-to-one*) if *every* element in $X$ is mapped to a *distinct* element in $Y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have $f(2)=2(-2)+1=-3$ and $f(4)=2\\cdot 4+1=9.$\n\n- Statement II is true. As we can see from the graph, it passes the horizontal line test, meaning that there are no two distinct elements within $[-2,4]$ that map to the same element in $[-3,9].$ So, for all ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ contained within the domain $[-2,4],$ if $f({x}_{1})$ and $f({x}_{2})$ are equal, then we must have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since statement II is true, $f$ is an injection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407919 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152201 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f45ad8ed-5b6d-5e46-b337-02ab271013b3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Injections From Functions Given Algebraically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34cfe54f-4c1a-5601-9bd2-0f1892a607c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407892} {:answer/id #uuid "60027804-dbc8-5ba3-b7bd-f44642116bde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407898} {:answer/id #uuid "8c1d872d-1050-528d-989c-6e743504ef10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407903} {:answer/id #uuid "965d76be-2ba3-58ed-b51e-9ca0ad934ed0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407904} {:answer/id #uuid "ad5fab01-32db-5160-9eaf-32f4b30665c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407907}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60027804-dbc8-5ba3-b7bd-f44642116bde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407898} :answer-field/id #uuid "50962eb5-13e0-5527-99de-391f22e7ee03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407913}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "23e55f80-c083-57f4-820d-7aa2d67256ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-105210" :question/problem "Which of the following functions are injections?\n\n- $f:R\\to R$ defined by $f(x)=2x+3$\n- $g:R\\to R$ defined by $g(x)=(x-2)^{2}$\n- $h:R\\to R$ defined by $h(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$" :question/worked-solution "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is a **injection** (also known as **one-to-one**) if *every* element in $x$ in $X$ is mapped to a *distinct* element in $Y.$\n\nSymbolically, we can write:\n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\n- I is an injection: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({x}_{1}) & =f({x}_{2}) \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+3 & =2{x}_{2}+3 \\\\ 2{x}_{1} & =2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{1} & ={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- II is *not*  an injection.  For example, $g(1)=1=g(3),$ but $1\\ne 3.$\n\n- III is *not* an injection. For example, $h(0)=1=h(2\\pi ),$ but $0\\ne 2\\pi .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152825 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152202 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a347133c-5e5a-5dd2-89ef-bdd14245ee6b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Injections For Functions Defined on the Set of Real Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02c08a79-bba6-544e-a9ea-ca58b06c0451" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407888} {:answer/id #uuid "8adf8b3d-ac7a-52cf-8b4c-03f7c2608d36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407901} {:answer/id #uuid "9d38aa51-050c-524f-9c39-3aa01c15091e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407906} {:answer/id #uuid "dae716d0-1847-54c9-9892-a356fbf81aea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407911} {:answer/id #uuid "e4388827-d9f0-5814-8367-e64cdc9001a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407912}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8adf8b3d-ac7a-52cf-8b4c-03f7c2608d36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407901} :answer-field/id #uuid "67bb5409-539d-5019-8102-b7a8fb7859d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407915}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0c274592-717b-5063-8a47-5f35487eca60" :question/math-academy-id "q-80091" :question/problem "![](images/9d/9df0c9dd8b76ac337b6110fc92019924ad2ee1c4cc0da8216c27709b641a8451.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:R\\to R$ whose graph is shown above?\n\n- $f(-2)\\ne f(0)$\n- $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in R,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}$\n- $f$ is **one-to-one**" :question/worked-solution "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is an injection (also known as *one-to-one*) if *every* element in $X$ is mapped to a *distinct* element in $Y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the graph, we have $f(-2)=f(0)=2.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Again, we have $f(-2)=f(0)$ but $-2\\ne 0.$ So, it's *not true* that for all ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ contained within the domain $R,$ if $f({x}_{1})$ and $f({x}_{2})$ are equal, then we must have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, since statement II is false, $f$ is not an injection (also known as *one-to-one*).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407921 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152202 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a347133c-5e5a-5dd2-89ef-bdd14245ee6b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Injections For Functions Defined on the Set of Real Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21c583e6-ea60-5d7f-b515-6a1c05522f68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407890} {:answer/id #uuid "486699da-5c52-50b7-a5e8-06a33ea08707" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407895} {:answer/id #uuid "bbe41323-bdcb-5854-b702-4569e847d444" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407908} {:answer/id #uuid "c5ef0e88-3d5a-58fe-a094-21c1a2d