$ are equal, then we must have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since statement II is true, $f$ is an injection (also known as *one-to-one*).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407920 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152203 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "99c2db8c-670b-521b-8942-3b89f1e95d3f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Injections For Functions Whose Domain or Codomain Are Real Intervals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "036d9afb-4836-58e3-9109-9dd285d1e857" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407889} {:answer/id #uuid "3c173147-60ba-5870-bce4-c2f5db134f39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407893} {:answer/id #uuid "4a28a6ba-2618-5929-9239-4e604586dd15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407896} {:answer/id #uuid "8a08c9ad-68d3-5048-9aa1-f2e7ffb89cb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407900} {:answer/id #uuid "8affdd3e-63cb-5945-abff-fdb11ff377f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407902}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a08c9ad-68d3-5048-9aa1-f2e7ffb89cb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407900} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e246480-5cbb-5dda-adb1-b0ac60280c69" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407916}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4c2e3f55-ea9b-5b9e-badf-26b9e5d93c01" :question/math-academy-id "q-80384" :question/problem "![](images/b0/b0a08488ba59344033205903ca973870d0b10837295ced970bf307342e6f7241.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:[-3,3]\\to [-3,0]$ whose graph is shown above?\n\n- $f(-3)=f(3)$\n- $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in [-3,3],$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}$\n- $f$ is an injection" :question/worked-solution "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is an injection (also known as *one-to-one*) if *every* element in $X$ is mapped to a *distinct* element in $Y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. According to the graph, we have $f(-3)=f(3)=0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Again, we have $f(-3)=f(3)$ but $-3\\ne 3.$ So, it's *not true* that for all ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ contained within the domain $[-3,3],$ if $f({x}_{1})$ and $f({x}_{2})$ are equal, then we must have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, since statement II is false, $f$ is not an injection (also known as *one-to-one*).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}]}
